      "strengths_deep_dive": [
        "350.org maintains high efficiency across its advocacy programs. In FY2024, the organization achieved a <cite id=\"6\">Program expense ratio of 83.2%</cite>, which surpasses the advocacy sector median of 78.4% [6], [7]. Operational efficiency is further reinforced by low fundraising overhead, costing <cite id=\"7\">$0.12 per $1 raised</cite> [7]. Financial stability is solid, supported by <cite id=\"7\">7.2 months of working capital</cite> [7]. Independent evaluators recognize this performance, resulting in an <cite id=\"7\">overall score of 97.0/100 from Charity Navigator</cite> [7]. Transparency practices are also notable, as the charity holds a <cite id=\"3\">Platinum Seal of Transparency from Candid</cite> [3]."
      ],
      "summary": "350.org mobilizes global climate action through training and advocacy campaigns [3]. In FY2024, the organization managed <cite id=\"1\">Total Revenue of $19,507,313</cite> to support its global operations [6]. <cite id=\"7\">Founded in 2008</cite>, the entity focuses on movement building, resisting fossil fuel projects, and advancing renewable energy solutions [3]. The charity maintains strong operational health, reflected in an <cite id=\"7\">overall score of 97.0/100 from Charity Navigator</cite> [7]. In FY2024, the group allocated funds efficiently, achieving <cite id=\"6\">a Program expense ratio of 83.2%</cite> [6]. This beats the 75% industry standard and exceeds the peer program ratio median of 78.4% [6], [7]. Furthermore, fundraising costs remain low, with the organization <cite id=\"7\">spending only $0.12 per $1 raised</cite> [7]. Operational revenue has stayed roughly flat, shifting from $21,511,958 in 2022 to $19,507,313 in FY2024 [6]. Grantmaking forms an important part of its global strategy, distributing $1,330,692 in grants during FY2023 [6]. This included $565,000 to domestic partners and $765,692 to foreign entities to build local movement capacity [6].",
